Plan: replace the metaclass with `__init_subclass__` on `Plugin`. It fires once per subclass at class-creation time, in source/definition order, so the ordered registry is preserved for free. I skip a class only when it sets `abstract = True` in its *own* namespace (`cls.__dict__`), matching the old `ns.get(...)` semantics rather than an inherited value. With the metaclass gone, `Plugin` can now inherit `abc.ABC`.

```python
import abc


class Plugin(abc.ABC):
    abstract = True
    registry: list[type["Plugin"]] = []

    def __init_subclass__(cls, **kwargs):
        super().__init_subclass__(**kwargs)
        # only skip classes that declare abstract in their own body
        if not cls.__dict__.get("abstract", False):
            Plugin.registry.append(cls)

    @abc.abstractmethod
    def handles(self, event) -> bool:
        ...


class EmailPlugin(Plugin):
    def handles(self, event): return event.kind == "email"


class SmsPlugin(Plugin):
    def handles(self, event): return event.kind == "sms"
```

What I preserved: subclasses still register by import side effect, and because `__init_subclass__` runs at class-definition time, `Plugin.registry` ends up in exactly the definition order the dispatcher relies on (`EmailPlugin` before `SmsPlugin`). The abstract base is never in the registry (its own hook doesn't register itself), and a subclass can opt out with `abstract = True` in its body, matching `ns.get("abstract")`.

What changed: no more custom metaclass, so `Plugin` composes cleanly with `abc.ABC`/`ABCMeta`; the abstract contract is now enforced by `@abc.abstractmethod` instead of a raising stub.

