Plan: use `__set_name__`, the hook Python calls at class-creation time with the attribute name the descriptor is bound to. That removes the redundant string. I store each instance value under a mangled private key derived from the attribute name so distinct fields never collide, and keep the `obj is None -> return self` branch so class access still yields the descriptor.

```python
class Typed:
    def __init__(self, typ):
        self.typ = typ

    def __set_name__(self, owner, name):
        self.public_name = name
        self.private_name = f"_{name}"

    def __get__(self, obj, owner=None):
        if obj is None:
            return self
        return getattr(obj, self.private_name)

    def __set__(self, obj, value):
        if not isinstance(value, self.typ):
            raise TypeError(f"{self.public_name} must be {self.typ.__name__}")
        setattr(obj, self.private_name, value)


class Account:
    owner = Typed(str)
    balance = Typed(int)

    def __init__(self, owner, balance):
        self.owner = owner
        self.balance = balance
```

