```python
import functools


class Renderer:
    def __init__(self, indent="  ", dialect="ansi"):
        self.indent = indent
        self.dialect = dialect

    @functools.singledispatch
    def render(self, node):
        raise TypeError(f"no renderer for {type(node).__name__}")

    @render.register(int)
    def _render_int(self, node):
        return str(node)

    @render.register(str)
    def _render_str(self, node):
        return repr(node)

    @render.register(list)
    def _render_list(self, node):
        return "\n".join(self.indent + self.render(n) for n in node)
```

What I preserved: `render` is still a method with full access to `self.indent`/`self.dialect`, the outputs for `int`/`str`/`list` are identical, nested lists still recurse, and unknown node types raise the same `TypeError`.

What changed: the `if/elif isinstance` ladder is gone; node types are now registered handlers, so a team can add `@render.register(MyNode)` for a new node type without touching the core method.

